   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to directing the metropolitan transportation authority to\nstudy and report on the feasibility of rehabilitating the Lefferts Boulevard\nBridge",
    "basePrintNo": "S8029",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8029-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 16,
       "fullName": "Toby Ann Stavisky",
       "imgName": "400_toby_ann_stavisky.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 400,
       "sessionMemberId": 924,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "STAVISKY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Transportation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8029\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act in relation to directing the metropolitan\ntransportation authority to study and report on the feasibility of reha-\nbilitating the Lefferts Boulevard Bridge\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nDirects the metropolitan transportation authority to study and report on\nthe feasibility of rehabilitating the Lefferts Boulevard Bridge\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Directs the MTA to conduct a study on the feasibility of\nrehabilitation on the Lefferts Boulevard Bridge. The study shall focus\non the costs and benefits of rehabilitation as well as a) feasible\nalternatives to demolition, b) economic impact of alternatives, c)\neconomic impact to surrounding businesses if demolition occurs.\n\nSection 2: Directs MTA to submit written report of findings and recom-\nmendations to the governor, state legislature and mayor of New York\nCity.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSince 1925, the MTA has owned and operated the Lefferts Boulevard Bridge\nabove the Long Island Railroad line in Kew Gardens. Since its inception,\nthe authority has shirked its responsibility in proper maintenance of\nthe bridge. This inaction has resulted in the complete state of disre-\npair of the structure, jeopardizing the future of the neighborhood's\ncharacter and the 12 small businesses that currently operate at the\nsite. To date, the MTA has not offered a potential solution that would\ninclude the rehabilitation of the bridge without full demolition. This\nlegislation would direct the authority to conduct a study to explore\nalternatives to demolition and report on potential impacts to the local\nbusiness community. A study of this report shall be presented to the\ngovernor, state legislature and mayor of New York City by March 1, 2019.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
